<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dubrovnik</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dubrovnik is a city on the southern Dalmatian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, known historically as Ragusa. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1808 it was the centre of the independent maritime Republic of Ragusa, a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Its walled old town, largely rebuilt after an earthquake in 1667, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and was shelled during the siege of 1991–1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dubrovnik.jpg" alt="Dubrovnik" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zysko serhii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_walls_of_the_fortress_and_View_of_the_old_city._panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dubrovnik is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, on the Adriatic Sea, a seaport and the centre of Dubrovnik-Neretva County. Its population was 41,562 in 2021. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed the Old City of Dubrovnik as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1979, recognising its medieval architecture and fortifications. The two names, Dubrovnik and Ragusa, co-existed for centuries. Ragusa, recorded since at least the 10th century, was the official name of the Republic of Ragusa until 1808, while Dubrovnik, first recorded in the Charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-kulin">Ban Kulin</a> in 1189, was in widespread use by the late 16th or early 17th century and is usually explained from a Slavic word for oak wood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was inhabited in ancient times by the Illyrian tribe of the Pleraei. According to Constantine Porphyrogenitus, writing around 950, Ragusa was founded in the 7th century by refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Epidaurum</a>, a Roman city about 15 kilometres to the south destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> fighting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>. After the fall of the Ostrogothic kingdom the town came under Byzantine protection. In the 12th and 13th centuries it grew into an oligarchic republic and was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1205 to 1358. A fire destroyed most of the city on 16 August 1296, and a new urban plan followed. By the Peace Treaty of Zadar in 1358 it gained relative independence as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>.</p><p>Between the 14th and 19th centuries Dubrovnik ruled itself as a free state, prosperous from maritime trade, with its highest development in the 15th and 16th centuries, when it was noted for wealth and skilled diplomacy and was a cradle of Croatian literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was the official language until 1472, although most of the population spoke Croatian. An earthquake in 1667 almost destroyed the whole city. French forces occupied the republic on 27 May 1806, and in 1808 Marshal Marmont abolished it and joined its territory to the Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After 1815 Dubrovnik belonged to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> within the Habsburg Empire. In 1918 it became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was in the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and afterwards in the Socialist Republic of Croatia within Yugoslavia. In 1991, in the Croatian War of Independence, the Yugoslav People's Army besieged the city and shelled it, causing significant damage. After repair and restoration it became one of the best-known tourist destinations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, often called the Queen of the Adriatic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dubrovnik” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Croatian University in Zagreb was the only university, and four state theatres played in Zagreb, Osijek, <b>Dubrovnik</b> and Sarajevo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ragusa, the Croatian <b>Dubrovnik</b>, grew up on a rocky islet off the southern Dalmatian coast; according to tradition it was founded in the seventh century by refugees from the Roman town of Epidaurum (modern Cavtat), destroyed by Avars and Slavs, and a Slavic settlement grew up on the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…as Varna, Mesembria (Nesebar) and Anchialos linked the country to wider trade, and Ivan Asen II granted merchants from Ragusa (<b>Dubrovnik</b>) a charter allowing them to trade freely in his lands; Venetians and Genoese became prominent in the fourteenth century, when the tsars issued silver coinage in quantity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with Venice were resumed, and the port of Ragusa (<b>Dubrovnik</b>) became an important point of exchange; Ragusan merchants held trading privileges throughout the realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With war looming, the regent Prince Paul sought agreement with the Croatian Peasant Party, and the Cvetković-Maček Agreement of 26 August 1939 merged the Sava and Littoral banovinas with parts of Bosnia, Herzegovina, Srem and the <b>Dubrovnik</b> area into one Banovina of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/state/banovina-of-croatia">Banovina of Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>Travunia formed in the 7th to 9th centuries among Serbs settled in the hinterland of <b>Dubrovnik</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/travunia">Travunia</a></li></ul></section>
